---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const results = [
  { value: "Google AI Overview", label: "ranked within 1 week" },
  { value: "Real inbound RFQ", label: "direct revenue generated" },
  { value: "100% inbox", label: "cold-email deliverability" },
  { value: "11 pages", label: "rebuilt with full schema" },
];
const stack = [
  "WordPress REST API",
  "Python automation",
  "JSON-LD schema",
  "Google Workspace",
  "Apollo.io",
  "Smartlead",
  "DKIM / SPF / DMARC",
  "GA4 + Search Console",
];
const title = "Kansas Castings — AI-Driven Growth Engine";
const desc =
  "How I took a 30-year iron foundry from invisible to ranking in Google's AI Overview — and built the lead pipeline behind it.";
---

<CaseStudy
  title={title}
  desc={desc}
  label="Case Study · Growth Engineering"
  heading="Kansas Castings"
  results={results}
  stack={stack}
>
  <section class="block">
    <h2>The challenge</h2>
    <p>
      Kansas Castings is a 30-year-old American iron foundry that pours specialty
      high-chrome white iron. The product was excellent; the web presence wasn't.
      The site was thin and unranked, there was no inbound pipeline, and the
      company was effectively invisible for the exact searches its buyers run —
      phrases like "white iron castings supplier." The brief: turn the site into
      something that finds customers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>What I built</h2>
    <h3>Technical SEO &amp; content</h3>
    <p>
      I rebuilt the site end-to-end through the WordPress REST API and Python
      scripts — rewriting titles, meta, and on-page copy across all 11 pages, and
      fixing a site-wide missing-H1 problem at the template level. Every page got
      accurate, buyer-intent content instead of filler.
    </p>
    <h3>Structured data</h3>
    <p>
      I shipped a full JSON-LD schema stack — Organization, LocalBusiness,
      Product, and FAQ markup — so search engines (and AI answer engines) could
      actually understand what the foundry makes, where, and for whom. This is the
      layer that made the AI Overview placement possible.
    </p>
    <h3>Lead capture</h3>
    <p>
      I built a custom RFQ quote form with server-side handling, email routing,
      and a database backup of every submission — debugging a hosting WAF quirk
      and SMTP authentication along the way so quotes actually land in the owner's
      inbox.
    </p>
    <h3>Cold-outbound system</h3>
    <p>
      Beyond inbound, I stood up a full outbound engine: a dedicated sending
      domain on Google Workspace with DKIM/SPF/DMARC and a warm-up schedule,
      Apollo.io for targeted prospecting by vertical, and Smartlead running
      multi-step sequences — reaching 100% inbox placement in deliverability
      testing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      Within a week of launch, Kansas Castings surfaced in <strong>Google's AI
      Overview</strong> for a high-intent buyer query — alongside far larger
      national foundries. The SEO work produced a <strong>real inbound RFQ</strong>:
      direct revenue, not a vanity metric. The outbound system gave the owner a
      repeatable way to reach new buyers on top of the inbound the site now
      generates.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
